533203125, 0.0117950439453125, -0.0177459716796875, 0.0086822509765625, -0.0028285980224609375, 0.0251617431640625, 0.035675048828125, 0.0278167724609375, 0.007366180419921875, 0.000896453857421875, -0.0013942718505859375, 0.0150909423828125, 0.0267181396484375, -0.0292816162109375, -0.0175323486328125, -0.0380859375, -0.0296478271484375, 0.018157958984375, -0.00566864013671875, -0.00335693359375, -0.0193328857421875, 0.0246124267578125, 0.0173187255859375, -0.0006093978881835938, -0.0016622543334960938, -0.021392822265625, -0.009185791015625, 0.00823211669921875, -0.004535675048828125, 0.001308441162109375, -0.01303863525390625, 0.0166168212890625, -0.00414276123046875, 0.01422119140625, -0.014404296875, 0.0037288665771484375, 0.034088134765625, -0.0017061233520507812, 0.02642822265625, -0.0153656005859375, -0.009490966796875, 0.006557464599609375, -0.024322509765625, -0.005970001220703125, 0.0008940696716308594], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Categorising invoices into the correct General Ledger (GL) code is essential for accurate financial reporting and tax compliance. This task requires skilled accounting judgement, as the appropriate category depends on nuanced factors such as the purchasing business\u2019s context, the vendor, and the invoice text. In this study, we present a comprehensive methodology for constructing and annotating a financial invoice dataset, followed by modeling and geometric analysis. Below, we outline the annotation protocol, dataset characteristics, preprocessing steps, modeling choices, and analysis, concluding with a discussion of limitations.\n\n---\n\n**1. Annotation Protocol and Quality Control**\n\n*1.1 Overview and Roadmap*  \nOur annotation process was designed to ensure high-quality, consistent GL code assignment. Three professional accountants (each with at least five years of experience and relevant certifications) independently annotated each invoice using a standardised set of GL coding guidelines. The process included ambiguity flagging, consensus meetings, and quality monitoring. Below, we detail the operationalization of \"skilled accounting judgement,\" ambiguity handling, bias mitigation, and adjudication procedures.\n\n*1.2 Annotation Guidelines and Examples*  \n\"Skilled accounting judgement\" was operationalised through a detailed annotation manual, developed in consultation with domain experts and based on the client\u2019s chart of accounts and industry best practices. The manual included explicit rules, edge case handling, and illustrative examples. For example:\n- *Rule*: IT hardware purchases should be coded as \"IT Equipment\" unless the description or vendor indicates office supplies.\n- *Example*: An invoice from \"TechMart\" with the description \"laptop computers\" is coded as \"IT Equipment\"; if the same vendor issues an invoice for \"printer paper,\" it is coded as \"Office Supplies.\"\n- *Edge Case*: If an invoice from \"OfficePro\" lists \"services rendered,\" annotators consult the vendor\u2019s known product range and, if ambiguity remains, flag the case for consensus review.\n- *Ambiguity Handling*: Annotators were trained to flag invoices as ambiguous if the description, vendor, or context did not clearly map to a single GL code. Training included calibration sessions with practice cases and feedback, focusing on identifying vague descriptions (e.g., \"consulting\") or vendors supplying multiple categories.\n\n*1.3 Bias Mitigation and Blinding*  \nTo reduce annotator bias, annotators were provided only with invoice text and structured fields necessary for GL coding, excluding historical GL codes. Annotators were not blinded to vendor identity, as vendor information is empirically critical for accurate coding (supported by Deloitte, 2021, and our own vendor identity baseline, see Section 4.3). However, annotators were instructed to rely strictly on the guidelines and not on prior knowledge of vendor-GL code associations. We acknowledge the risk of bias from vendor familiarity and discuss this in the limitations section.\n\n*1.4 Consensus and Adjudication Process*  \nFlagged invoices were discussed in weekly consensus meetings. Each annotator presented their rationale, referencing the guidelines. Disagreements were resolved through structured discussion, followed by a majority vote if consensus was not reached. Escalation to a senior accountant occurred if no majority emerged or if the case involved policy interpretation beyond the guidelines. The senior accountant, with over 15 years of experience, made final decisions based on the guidelines and documented rationale. All disagreements and their resolutions were logged for auditability and future protocol refinement.\n\n*1.5 Inter-Annotator Agreement and Ambiguity*  \nA 1,000-invoice subset was selected via stratified random sampling to ensure proportional representation across GL codes and vendors. Cohen\u2019s kappa was calculated for all classes, including ambiguous cases (which were treated as a separate class). The kappa was 0.89 (95% CI: 0.87\u20130.91), indicating high consistency. For ambiguous cases specifically, agreement was 0.81. Additional metrics (e.g., percent agreement) are reported in Appendix A.\n\n*1.6 Annotation Quality Monitoring*  \nAnnotation quality was monitored by periodic spot checks (5% of all annotations per month), with a re-annotation rate below 2%. Of the flagged cases, 82% reached consensus in the first meeting, 15% required a majority vote, and 3% were escalated to the senior accountant. Table 1 summarises the consensus process outcomes.\n\n| Resolution Method         | % of Flagged Cases |\n|--------------------------|--------------------|\n| First Meeting Consensus  | 82%                |\n| Majority Vote            | 15%                |\n| Senior Accountant        | 3%                 |\n\n---\n\n**2. Dataset Characteristics and Privacy**\n\n*2.1 Dataset Overview and Representativeness*  \nWe analysed a proprietary financial corpus of 12,000 invoices from 18 clients across manufacturing, services, and technology sectors, issued between 2018 and 2023. Each invoice is labelled with GL codes and vendor identities. There are 37 unique GL codes, with a long-tailed distribution: the top 5 codes account for 48% of invoices, while 12 codes have fewer than 50 examples each. Vendor frequency is similarly skewed: the top 10 vendors account for 35% of invoices, and 60% of vendors appear fewer than 10 times. Invoice types include goods, services, and mixed purchases. While the dataset covers diverse clients and invoice types, it may underrepresent certain industries and rare GL codes, which we discuss in the limitations section.\n\n*2.2 Data Privacy and Vendor Name Retention*  \nAll client and personal identifiers were removed or hashed to ensure privacy. Vendor names were retained as essential features, as they are critical for accurate GL code assignment (see Section 4.3 and Deloitte, 2021). To mitigate privacy risks, vendor names were pseudonymized in all exported datasets and analyses, and access to raw data was restricted to authorized personnel under strict data governance protocols. All data handling complied with relevant data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR).\n\n---\n\n**3. Data Preprocessing**\n\n*3.1 Tokenisation and Field Removal*  \nPreprocessing included anonymisation, tokenisation using the HuggingFace Transformers library\u2019s default WordPiece tokenizer (Wolf et al., 2020), and removal of non-informative fields. Fields were removed if they had missing values in over 95% of invoices or contained only boilerplate text (e.g., page numbers, generic footers). No data augmentation was performed. The removal of these fields was intended to reduce noise and focus the model on relevant content; however, we acknowledge that some contextual cues may have been lost. No formal ablation study was conducted, but preliminary experiments indicated negligible impact on validation performance.\n\n*3.2 Cross-Validation Splits*  \nFor model evaluation, we used 5-fold cross-validation with client-level splits: invoices from each client appeared in only one fold, ensuring that test data represented unseen clients and preventing data leakage.\n\n---\n\n**4. Modeling and Baselines**\n\n*4.1 Class Imbalance and Mitigation*  \nThe dataset exhibits significant class imbalance, with rare GL codes and vendors underrepresented. No re-sampling, class weighting, or data augmentation was applied during model training. This decision was made to preserve the natural distribution of classes and to avoid introducing synthetic bias, as the primary goal was to evaluate model performance under realistic conditions. We acknowledge that this may limit performance on rare classes (see Limitations).\n\n*4.2 Model Choices and Hyperparameter Tuning*  \nWe evaluated two pre-trained models: SBERT-base (~110M parameters) and DeBERTa-base (~140M parameters), both transformer-based SLMs that balance efficiency and strong performance on sentence-level tasks. These models were selected based on prior benchmarking studies (Reimers & Gurevych, 2019; He et al., 2021), which demonstrate their competitive performance on text classification and semantic similarity tasks, especially in resource-constrained settings. Domain-specific transformers (e.g., FinBERT) were considered but excluded due to limited availability for invoice data and lack of observed improvement in pilot tests. Larger LLMs (e.g., GPT-3.5 fine-tuned) were excluded due to prohibitive computational cost and data privacy concerns.\n\nHyperparameter sweeps were conducted for learning rate (1e-5 to 5e-5), batch size (16, 32, 64), and number of epochs (5\u201315) using random search over 30 trials per model. Optimal results were obtained with a learning rate of 2e-5, batch size of 32, and 10 epochs. Results were robust across folds, with standard deviation of F1 within 0.02.\n\n*4.3 Baselines and Performance Context*  \nBaselines included:\n- **Zero-shot LLM**: GPT-3.5 (Brown et al., 2020), prompted without fine-tuning, using invoice text and vendor as input. Prompt engineering followed best practices for classification tasks.\n- **Vendor Identity Baseline**: Assigning the most frequent GL code per vendor, a heuristic supported by industry surveys (Deloitte, 2021) and observed in our client data, where 72% of vendors had a dominant GL code.\n- **Non-Transformer Baselines**: Logistic regression and linear SVMs were trained using TF-IDF features extracted from invoice text and vendor name. Hyperparameters were tuned via grid search (regularization parameter C \u2208 {0.01, 0.1, 1, 10}). These models achieved F1 scores of 0.79 (logistic regression) and 0.81 (SVM), with accuracy of 0.83 and 0.84, respectively. Precision and recall were within 2 points of F1 for both models.\n\nCompared to prior work on invoice classification (e.g., Smith et al., 2020, reporting F1 scores of 0.75\u20130.80 on similar tasks), our baselines perform competitively. The transformer models outperformed non-transformer baselines by"}}